 In C programming, there are several ways to return an error, but one common approach is to use error codes. Here's an example of a function that calculates the factorial of a number and returns an error code if the input is negative:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int factorial(int n) {
    if (n < 0) {
        return -1; // Error code for negative input
    }

    int result = 1;
    for (int i = 2; i <= n; ++i) {
        result *= i;
    }

    return result;
}

int main() {
    int num = 5;
    int result = factorial(num);

    if (result == -1) {
        printf("Error: Negative input is not allowed.\n");
    } else {
        printf("Factorial of %d is %d\n", num, result);
    }

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, the `factorial` function returns an error code (-1) when the input is negative. The `main` function checks the return value of the `factorial` function and handles the error accordingly. This approach allows for better error handling and makes it easier to identify and fix issues in your code.

